.TH BCC09 1 2026-11-12 "bcc09 0.15.0" "bcc09 Manual"
.SH NAME
bcc09 \- C compiler for the Motorola 6809
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bcc09
.RB [ \-c | \-S | \-E | \-P ]
.RB [ \-O ]
.RB [ \-M\c
.IR target ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR output ]
.RI [ options ]
.IR file ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bcc09
compiles C for the Motorola MC6809: the Tandy Color Computer and the
Dragon under Color BASIC, OS-9, FLEX, and machines of one's own.
It is a driver: it runs the preprocessor
.RB ( cpp ),
.B unproto
(which turns ANSI C prototypes into what the compiler reads), the
compiler
.RB ( cc1 ),
the peephole optimizer
.RB ( copt ,
with
.BR \-O ),
the assembler
.BR as09 (1)
and the linker
.BR ld09 (1),
as each file needs, keeping the intermediate files in a temporary
directory.
By default it links a program with the start-up code and C library of
the target that
.B \-M
chooses.
.PP
The C is C89's, with a 16-bit
.B int
and pointers, a 32-bit
.BR long ,
IEEE single and double
.B float
and
.BR double ,
and an unsigned
.BR char ;
.B _Bool
is C99's.
The compiler doesn't know
.B const
and
.BR volatile :
the C library's headers define them away (in features.h), so a program
that uses them must include one of the headers (or
.BI \-D const=\c
).
.PP
A file's suffix says what it is:
.TP
.B .c
C source.
.TP
.B .i
C already preprocessed.
.TP
.B .s
Assembly, for
.BR as09 (1).
.TP
.BR .S ", " .x
Assembly to be preprocessed first.
.TP
anything else
An object file or library, for the linker.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "What to do"
.TP
.B \-c
Compile or assemble each file to an object file
.RI ( name .o),
without linking.
.TP
.B \-S
Compile each C file to assembly
.RI ( name .s).
.TP
.B \-E
Preprocess each file, to standard output (or the
.B \-o
file).
.TP
.B \-P
Preprocess, without the line numbers.
.TP
.BI \-o " output"
The output file: the program (a.out by default), or, with
.BR \-c ,
.B \-S
or
.BR \-E ,
the one file's.
.TP
.B \-O
Optimize: run each compiled file through
.BR copt ,
with its 6809 rules.
.BI \-O name
also uses the rules in the file
.RI rules. name .
.SS Targets
.TP
.BI \-M target
The machine and system the program is for.
Each defines a macro, and links its own start-up code and library:
.RS
.TP
.B s
Standalone (the default): returns to its loader (a monitor, or Color
BASIC's EXEC); no I/O.
Defines
.BR __STANDALONE_ .
.TP
.B e
Color BASIC (or the Dragon's), loaded with LOADM or CLOADM and run with
EXEC: I/O through the ROM's POLCAT and CHROUT, and, with Disk BASIC on
a CoCo, its files.
Defines
.BR __CB__ .
.TP
.B r
A ROM cartridge for the CoCo (1 and 2) and the Dragon, which BASIC
starts at $C000: the output is the ROM's image, laid out by the linker
script
.I rom.ld
(the code and an image of the initialized data in the ROM, the data,
BSS and heap in RAM from $0600, the stack below $8000), and I/O through
the ROM's POLCAT and CHROUT, as with
.BR e ;
the start-up code copies the data to RAM, and
.B _exit
stops the program.
Defines
.B __CB__
and
.BR __CBROM__ .
.TP
.B o
OS-9 (and NitrOS-9): a position-independent program module, with its
data reached through the U register (make the module with
.BR "objcopy09 \-O os9" ).
Defines
.BR __OS9__ .
.TP
.B f
FLEX: a command, a .CMD file
.RB ( "objcopy09 \-O flex" ),
with FLEX's terminal and files.
Defines
.B __FLEX__
and
.BR __FLEX9__ .
.TP
.B v
A Vectrex cartridge: the output is the ROM's image, laid out by the
linker script
.I vectrex.ld
(the cartridge header at $0000, then the code and an image of the
initialized data; the data, BSS and stack in the Vectrex's RAM, $C880
to $CBEA), with the BIOS's routines for drawing, the controllers and
sound in
.I vectrex.h
and no other I/O; the start-up code copies the data to RAM, and
.B _exit
stops the program.
The header (the title the BIOS shows) is libvec.a's, unless a module
of the program's own defines
.B __vec_header
in segment 12.
Defines
.BR __VECTREX__ .
.TP
.B i
sim6809, the simulator bcc09's tests use.
Defines
.BR __SIM6809__ .
.TP
.BR u ", " b
Color BASIC USR functions and Disk BASIC: they define
.B __CB__
but have no start-up code or library yet, so only
.B \-x
links them, with the C library alone.
.RE
.TP
.B \-x
Link no start-up code: the program brings its own (the C library, and
the target's library if it has one, are still linked).
.SS "Passing options on"
.TP
.BI \-D name\c
.RI [= value ]
.TQ
.BI \-U name
.TQ
.BI \-I dir
Define or undefine a macro, or add an include directory, as
.BR bcc09\-cpp (1)
does.
.TP
.B \-I
(alone) Don't search the default include directory.
.TP
.BI \-L dir
Add a library directory for the linker.
.TP
.B \-L
(alone) Don't search the default library directory.
.TP
.BI \-l name
Link the library
.RI lib name .a.
The linker's options
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-m ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-d ,
.B \-y
and
.BI \-H size
go to it too (see
.BR ld09 (1));
its others, such as
.BI \-T addr\c
, which
.B bcc09
uses for itself, are given with
.BR \-X .
.TP
.BI \-X option
Pass
.I option
to the linker, for example
.B \-X\-T0x3000
for the program's load address, or
.B \-X\-Sprog.ld
for a linker script (see
.BR ld09 (1)).
.TP
.BI \-A option
Pass
.I option
to the assembler.
.TP
.BI \-t " seg"
Pass
.BI \-t " seg"
to the assembler.
.TP
.BI \-C option
Pass
.I option
to the compiler
.RB ( cc1 ).
.TP
.BI \-P option
Pass
.I option
to the preprocessor.
.TP
.BI \-u option
Pass
.I option
to
.BR unproto .
.TP
.B \-W
Show the assembler's warnings.
.TP
.B \-kr
Don't run
.B unproto
(for K&R C), and don't define
.BR __STDC__ .
.TP
.B \-ansi
Accepted and ignored (unproto is the default).
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-p ", " \-w
Accepted and ignored.
.SS "Where things are"
.TP
.BI \-B prefix
Look for the passes in
.I prefix
first.
.TP
.BI \-M /dir
The directory bcc09 is installed in, as
.B BCC_PREFIX
gives it.
.TP
.B \-M\-
Find it from where the
.B bcc09
program is: its directory, less its
.IR bin .
.TP
.BI \-T dir
The directory for temporary files (/tmp by default).
.TP
.B \-v
Print each command as it is run; twice, the temporary files' removal
too; three times, the commands without running them.
With no files, print the version (as in
.BR "bcc09 0.15.0" )
and the usage message.
.TP
.B \-V
Print each file's name as it is compiled.
.TP
.B \-e
Use the compiler's own preprocessor in place of
.BR cpp .
.SH MACROS
.B bcc09
defines
.BR __BCC__ ,
.BR __6809__ ,
.B __STDC__
(unless
.BR \-kr ),
and the target's macro (see
.BR \-M ).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BCC_PREFIX
The directory bcc09 is installed in: it runs its passes from
.IR libexec/bcc09 ,
and finds the C library's headers in
.I share/bcc09/include
and its libraries in
.I share/bcc09
under it.
By default, the directory bcc09 was built to be installed in
.RB ( "make PREFIX=" ...;
/usr/local unless that says otherwise).
.TP
.B BCC_EXEC_PREFIX
Another directory to look for the passes in.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IR prefix /libexec/bcc09
The passes:
.BR cpp ,
.BR unproto ,
.BR cc1 ,
.B copt
and its rules (rules.6809).
.TP
.IR prefix /share/bcc09
The C library (libc.a), and each target's start-up code and library
(crt0.o and libsa.a for
.BR \-Ms ;
crt0.o and libcb.a for
.BR \-Me ;
crtrom.o, librom.a and the linker script rom.ld for
.BR \-Mr ;
crtvec.o, libvec.a and vectrex.ld for
.BR \-Mv ;
crtos9.o and libos9.a, all of libc built for OS-9, for
.BR \-Mo ;
crtflex.o and libflex.a for
.BR \-Mf ;
crtsim.o and libsim.a for
.BR \-Mi ).
.TP
.IR prefix /share/bcc09/include
The C library's headers.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 if every file was compiled (and the program linked), 1 otherwise.
.SH EXAMPLES
A Color BASIC program, loaded at $3000, on a cassette:
.PP
.RS
.nf
bcc09 \-Me \-O \-o hello \-X\-T0x3000 hello.c
objcopy09 \-O cas \-n HELLO hello hello.cas
.fi
.RE
.PP
A ROM cartridge's image (for XRoar's
.BR \-cart\-rom ,
or an EPROM):
.PP
.RS
.nf
bcc09 \-Mr \-O \-o hello.rom hello.c
.fi
.RE
.PP
A Vectrex cartridge, with a title of its own in
.I title.s
(see
.IR vectrex.h ):
.PP
.RS
.nf
bcc09 \-Mv \-O \-o game.bin game.c title.s
.fi
.RE
.PP
An OS-9 program module:
.PP
.RS
.nf
bcc09 \-Mo \-O \-o hello.out hello.c
objcopy09 \-O os9 \-n hello hello.out hello
.fi
.RE
.SH NOTES
OS-9's, Color BASIC's and FLEX's lines end with a carriage return,
and the C library passes bytes as they are: a program writes
\(dq\er\(dq (on FLEX, \(dq\er\en\(dq), and reads such lines with
.BR getdelim (3)
and \(aq\er\(aq.
The C library's README describes each target in full.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR as09 (1),
.BR ld09 (1),
.BR ar09 (1),
.BR objdump09 (1),
.BR objcopy09 (1);
the passes:
.BR bcc09\-cpp (1),
.BR bcc09\-unproto (1),
.BR bcc09\-cc1 (1),
.BR bcc09\-copt (1)
